Reset the user phrase query in UserGetPhraseEnd()

UserGetPhraseEnd() did nothing, so a caller that stops at the first row,
e.g. FindInterval() checking whether a user phrase exists, left the query
at a row. The connection then keeps a read lock on the database, every
other connection's COMMIT fails with SQLITE_BUSY, and since
UserUpdatePhraseEnd() ignores that, their transactions stay open and
chewing_new2() fails for all new contexts.

PIME opens one context per input client in a single backend process, so
this locks up new clients until the backend restarts.

Written with assistance from Claude Code (Claude Opus 5.5).

--- a/src/userphrase-sql.c
+++ b/src/userphrase-sql.c
@@ -441,9 +441,21 @@
     return &pgdata->userphrase_data;
 }
 
-void UserGetPhraseEnd(ChewingData *pgdata UNUSED, const uint16_t phoneSeq[] UNUSED)
+void UserGetPhraseEnd(ChewingData *pgdata, const uint16_t phoneSeq[] UNUSED)
 {
-    /* FIXME: Remove this */
+    int ret;
+
+    assert(pgdata);
+
+    /*
+     * Callers may stop before the last row. A query left at a row keeps a
+     * read lock on the database, and then the commits of every other
+     * connection to it fail.
+     */
+    ret = sqlite3_reset(pgdata->static_data.stmt_userphrase[STMT_USERPHRASE_SELECT_BY_PHONE]);
+    if (ret != SQLITE_OK) {
+        LOG_ERROR("sqlite3_reset returns %d", ret);
+    }
 }
 
